WEBVTT

00:00:00.440 --> 00:00:04.550
Private RAG architecture starts with source control and ends with governed operation.

00:00:04.550 --> 00:00:07.160
The system should preserve document boundaries during ingestion,

00:00:07.160 --> 00:00:11.700
keep chunks attached to metadata and provenance, and maintain lifecycle status so old,

00:00:11.700 --> 00:00:15.400
restricted, or tenant-specific content does not become anonymous text.

00:00:15.400 --> 00:00:18.275
Permissions must follow the user and workflow into retrieval.

00:00:18.275 --> 00:00:21.945
A private RAG system should filter by identity, tenant, document access,

00:00:21.945 --> 00:00:25.960
source eligibility, and application context before evidence reaches the model.

00:00:25.960 --> 00:00:29.740
Retrieved content should remain separate from trusted instructions and should be treated

00:00:29.740 --> 00:00:31.425
as evidence rather than authority.

00:00:31.425 --> 00:00:35.825
Quality control is ongoing. Teams should evaluate retrieval, groundedness, citations,

00:00:35.825 --> 00:00:38.400
freshness, no-answer behavior, and user feedback.

00:00:38.400 --> 00:00:42.210
Logs can improve operations, but they also need redaction, retention limits,

00:00:42.210 --> 00:00:46.265
and access controls because prompts and responses may contain sensitive material.

00:00:46.265 --> 00:00:48.355
Finally, operate the system over time.

00:00:48.355 --> 00:00:52.760
Plan refresh, deletion, backups, versioning, rollback, and incident response.

00:00:52.760 --> 00:00:56.160
A private RAG system succeeds when users can trust not only the answer,

00:00:56.160 --> 00:00:59.840
but also the source, permission boundary, and operational process behind it.

00:00:59.840 --> 00:01:02.680
The practical habit is to design for review from the beginning.

00:01:02.680 --> 00:01:06.890
If a team can explain the source, access decision, retrieval path, answer evidence,

00:01:06.890 --> 00:01:10.800
and maintenance plan, the RAG system is much easier to improve safely.
